# Project Rules: Clean Code and SOLID (strictly enforced)

These rules apply to every file in this monorepo (Rust and TypeScript) and must be
followed for all new code and all refactors.

## 1. One responsibility per file
- NEVER dump multiple responsibilities, structs, or classes into a single file.
  This applies above all to `lib.rs`, `index.ts` and `main.ts`.
- One primary struct / class / module per file. Small private helpers that exist
  only to serve that primary type may live next to it; anything else gets its own file.
- Unit tests live in the same file as the type they test (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests`).

## 2. SOLID, especially the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
- SRP: every struct, class, function and module has exactly one reason to change.
- Open/Closed: extend behaviour by adding new types or modules, not by growing
  existing ones with flags and special cases.
- Liskov / Interface Segregation: keep traits and interfaces small and focused;
  never force a consumer to depend on methods it does not use.
- Dependency Inversion: depend on abstractions at module boundaries, and compose
  small types rather than inheriting or duplicating behaviour.

## 3. Entrypoints are barrels only
- `lib.rs` and `index.ts` must stay clean barrel / export entrypoints:
  `mod` / `pub mod` declarations and `pub use` / `export ... from` re-exports only.
- Zero business logic, zero data structures, zero FFI function bodies in entrypoints.
- `main.ts` only wires modules together; logic belongs in dedicated modules.

## 4. Directory layout by domain
Group related domain logic into dedicated subdirectories, each with a `mod.rs`
(Rust) or `index.ts` (TypeScript) barrel:
- `ecs/`       entity / component storage and indexing
- `math/`      pure mathematical logic (vectors, matrices, transforms)
- `renderer/`  rendering and GPU-facing code
- `memory/`    linear-memory and buffer interop helpers

## 5. Separate data, math and bindings
- Data structures, mathematical logic, and FFI / Wasm bindings must live in
  separate files.
- Keep domain logic pure (no `#[wasm_bindgen]`, no JS types) wherever possible,
  and wrap it in a thin binding layer that only translates types.
- Never duplicate logic (DRY): extract a small private helper instead.

## 6. Self-documenting code
- Use intention-revealing names; prefer small functions with a single purpose.
- Replace magic numbers with named constants.
- Comment the *why* (invariants, contracts, trade-offs), not the *what*.
- Document every public item and every module (`//!` / `///` / TSDoc).
